{"id":"h20t3","name":"Customers at Risk","lang":"sql","entry":null,"params":[],"limit_s":10,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>The table <b>orders</b> has the columns <code>order_id</code>, <code>customer_id</code>, <code>ordered_on</code> (date) and <code>status</code>. Orders with the status <code>'cancelled'</code> do not count. Several orders of a customer on the same day count as <b>one</b> ordering day.</p>\n<p>For a customer, the <b>gaps</b> are the numbers of days between two consecutive ordering days. Only customers with at least 3 ordering days are analysed. Their <b>usual gap</b> is the median of their gaps (continuous median, <code>percentile_cont(0.5)</code>).</p>\n<p>The report is made on the reference date <code>2026-12-31</code>. A customer is <b>at risk</b> when the number of days from their last ordering day to the reference date is strictly greater than twice their usual gap.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return the customers at risk with the columns <code>customer_id</code>, <code>ordering_days</code>, <code>usual_gap</code> (rounded to 1 decimal), <code>days_since_last</code> and <code>ratio</code> (<code>days_since_last / usual_gap</code>, rounded to 2 decimals), sorted by <code>ratio</code> from the highest, then <code>customer_id</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p>Table <code>orders</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>order_id</th><th>customer_id</th><th>ordered_on</th><th>status</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2026-11-01</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>2026-11-11</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>2026-11-21</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>2026-11-21</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>2026-01-01</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2</td><td>2026-03-01</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>2</td><td>2026-04-01</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>2</td><td>2026-04-02</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>3</td><td>2026-12-01</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>3</td><td>2026-12-30</td><td>cancelled</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>3</td><td>2026-12-11</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>3</td><td>2026-12-21</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>4</td><td>2026-12-01</td><td>paid</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>4</td><td>2026-12-10</td><td>paid</td></tr></table></div><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>customer_id</th><th>ordering_days</th><th>usual_gap</th><th>days_since_last</th><th>ratio</th></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>31.0</td><td>273</td><td>8.81</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>10.0</td><td>40</td><td>4.0</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 10 seconds (psql)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 1 GB<br><b>[input]</b> tables <code>orders</code><br><b>[output]</b> the result of one query with exactly the columns <code>customer_id</code>, <code>ordering_days</code>, <code>usual_gap</code>, <code>days_since_last</code>, <code>ratio</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"/* Write your PostgreSQL query here */\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["écarts entre commandes","médiane (percentile_cont)","plusieurs commandes le même jour","date de référence","HAVING"],"minutes":25,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>query.sql</code>. Efface le commentaire de départ si tu veux.</li><li>Écris <b>une seule requête</b> qui commence par <code>SELECT</code> (ou <code>WITH</code>). Les tables <code>orders</code> existent déjà, remplies avec les données du test : pas de <code>CREATE TABLE</code> ni d'<code>INSERT</code>.</li><li>Le moteur est PostgreSQL. Si tu écris plusieurs requêtes, seul le résultat de la dernière est noté.</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Le résultat de ta requête doit avoir <b>exactement 5 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>customer_id</code>, <code>ordering_days</code>, <code>usual_gap</code>, <code>days_since_last</code>, <code>ratio</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Nomme chaque colonne calculée avec <code>AS</code>.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>ORDER BY</code> à la fin de la requête).</li><li>Valeurs : les nombres sont comparés à 0,000001 près : arrondis seulement là où l'énoncé le demande, et au nombre de décimales demandé.</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 8 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"tables":{"orders":{"columns":[["order_id","INT"],["customer_id","INT"],["ordered_on","DATE"],["status","TEXT"]],"rows":[[1,1,"2026-11-01","paid"],[2,1,"2026-11-11","paid"],[3,1,"2026-11-21","paid"],[4,1,"2026-11-21","paid"],[5,2,"2026-01-01","paid"],[6,2,"2026-03-01","paid"],[7,2,"2026-04-01","paid"],[8,2,"2026-04-02","paid"],[9,3,"2026-12-01","paid"],[10,3,"2026-12-30","cancelled"],[11,3,"2026-12-11","paid"],[12,3,"2026-12-21","paid"],[13,4,"2026-12-01","paid"],[14,4,"2026-12-10","paid"]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["customer_id","ordering_days","usual_gap","days_since_last","ratio"],"rows":[[2,4,31.0,273,8.81],[1,3,10.0,40,4.0]]}},{"input":{"tables":{"orders":{"columns":[["order_id","INT"],["customer_id","INT"],["ordered_on","DATE"],["status","TEXT"]],"rows":[[1,7,"2026-12-01","paid"],[2,7,"2026-12-03","paid"],[3,7,"2026-12-05","paid"],[4,7,"2026-12-07","paid"],[5,8,"2026-12-01","paid"],[6,8,"2026-12-05","paid"],[7,8,"2026-12-11","paid"],[8,9,"2026-12-11","paid"],[9,9,"2026-12-16","paid"],[10,9,"2026-12-21","paid"]]}}},"expected":{"columns":["customer_id","ordering_days","usual_gap","days_since_last","ratio"],"rows":[[7,4,2.0,24,12.0],[8,3,5.0,20,4.0]]}}],"hidden_n":8,"hidden":"","solution":""}